     1	# Accounts Dashboard
     2	
     3	Accounts Dashboard is an interview starter with an Ember frontend, an API-only Rails backend, Pundit, PostgreSQL, and SCSS. Alex and Jordan are seeded demo users with separate accounts.
     4	
     5	## Run locally in VS Code
     6	
     7	Use Ruby 3.4.13, Node 22.12+ on the Node 22 release line, and a running Docker Desktop. `.nvmrc` lets `nvm install` pick a compatible Node version. If an existing VS Code terminal still finds the system Ruby, load the Mise setup with `source ~/.zprofile`.
     8	
     9	If you already have a local database, run `docker volume ls --filter label=com.docker.compose.volume=fieldwork_postgres` to find its volume. Set `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` to the prefix of that volume name before `_fieldwork_postgres` in the terminal where you run Compose so it reuses your data.
    10	
    11	1. Open the workspace root and run the following commands in its terminal.
    12	
    13	   ```sh
    14	   docker compose up -d --wait db
    15	   npm ci
    16	   ```
    17	
    18	2. Start the Rails JSON API in another terminal.
    19	
    20	   ```sh
    21	   cd backend
    22	   bundle install
    23	   bin/rails db:prepare
    24	   bin/rails db:seed
    25	   bin/rails server -b localhost
    26	   ```
    27	
    28	3. Run `npm start -- --host 127.0.0.1` from the workspace root and open [Accounts Dashboard](http://localhost:4200). Vite forwards `/api` to Rails on port 3000. Compose publishes PostgreSQL on `http://localhost:5433` with separate development and test databases.
    29	
    30	Alex is the default identity. Run `DEMO_USER_EMAIL=jordan@example.test bin/rails server -b 127.0.0.1` from `backend/` to practice as Jordan. The demo user comes from server configuration in development and test.
    31	
    32	Use `docker compose stop db` to stop the database and keep its data.
    33	
    34	## Walk through one transaction
    35	
    36	1. **Ember loads the screen.** The account route receives the ID from the URL and uses an injected API service to fetch the summary. The service loads Rails JSON into Ember Data records for users, accounts, and transactions. The account screen reads its balance and history from the account record. The form action reads `FormData` as strings. Tracked fields drive its saving, success, and error messages. [Route](app/routes/accounts/show.js) · [API service](app/services/accounts-api.js) · [Form](app/components/transaction-form.gjs)
    37	2. **Rails checks access.** A Pundit scope limits the SQL query to the current user’s accounts. A policy answers whether that user can perform an action on a particular record. A lookup outside the scope returns `404`, which also covers an ID that does not exist. [Account policy](backend/app/policies/account_policy.rb) · [Transaction policy](backend/app/policies/transaction_policy.rb)
    38	3. **PostgreSQL serializes writes to the account.** `with_lock` reloads the account and holds a row lock for the database transaction. The entry and balance update commit together. If a debit would overdraw the account, the balance validation raises and both writes roll back. Other processes using the same row must wait for the lock. A Ruby `Mutex` coordinates threads sharing that mutex in one process. [Write service](backend/app/services/post_transaction.rb)
    39	4. **Money stays exact.** PostgreSQL `decimal(19, 2)` allows 17 whole digits and two decimal places. Rails parses amount strings into `BigDecimal` and returns balances, totals, and amounts as strings. Ember adds display separators as text. After a save, the API service updates the existing account record and its transaction history from the server response. [Decimal helper](backend/app/models/usd_amount.rb) · [Summary service](backend/app/services/account_summary.rb) · [Ember display helper](app/helpers/usd.js)
    40	
    41	## API examples
    42	
    43	| Method and path                       | Response                                                           |
    44	| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
    45	| `GET /api/me`                         | Current demo user                                                  |
    46	| `GET /api/accounts`                   | Accounts in the current user’s scope                               |
    47	| `GET /api/accounts/:id`               | Balance, credit/debit totals, and newest-first transaction history |
    48	| `POST /api/accounts/:id/transactions` | Updated summary with HTTP `201`                                    |
    49	
    50	Send `{"transaction":{"direction":"credit","amount":"12.34","description":"Practice deposit"}}` as JSON. Error responses include `error.code` and `error.message`. The API uses `400` for an invalid request shape, `401` for a missing current user, `403` for a denied action, `404` for a missing or out-of-scope record, and `422` for invalid amounts or failed validations.
    51	
    52	Run `bin/rails test` from `backend/`. From the workspace root, `npm test` opens Chrome and keeps the frontend results visible. Press Enter in Testem to rerun or `q` to quit. Use `npm run test:ci` for a headless run that exits when the tests finish.
    53	
    54	The SCSS uses simple typography, grouped cards, and direct form labels inspired by Upstart’s public product pages. Tokens and layout rules live in one place. [Upstart personal loans](https://www.upstart.com/personal-loans) · [SCSS](app/styles/accounts-dashboard.scss)
